{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FqYz6ae4qewR44PVKPV7Uf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Specialized LoRA routing hypothesis fully validated — type-specific experts significantly outperform universal LoRA, and Meta-Brain reaches competitive absolute performance with 20x fewer parameters than VLA-GSE\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Perturbation-type-specific training data produces LoRA experts with clearly differentiated correction behaviors (specialization > generalization)\"\n      - \"PAR soft routing approximates oracle routing closely (gap < 2%), confirming Phase 0 signal is actionable\"\n      - \"Universal LoRA on pi0 saturates around 63-66% due to capacity dilution across perturbation types\"\n      - \"LoRA rank-16 on PaliGemma layers provides sufficient correction capacity per perturbation type\"\n    number_ranges:\n      libero_plus_overall_meta_brain: [76, 82]\n      gain_over_frozen: [22, 28]\n      universal_lora_overall: [63, 66]\n      gain_over_universal_lora: [10, 18]\n      per_type_specialization_delta_max: [12, 25]\n      oracle_par_gap: [0, 2]\n      routing_diagonality: [0.85, 0.98]\n      gain_per_million_params: [3.7, 4.7]\n      cm_ablation_delta: [1, 4]\n    narrative_direction: \"Original framing holds as-is. Headline: Meta-Brain achieves ~78% on LIBERO-Plus with 6M params, closing >80% of the gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params). All three claims land — diagnosability (Phase 0), targeted > uniform correction (Claim 2 ablation ladder), and parameter-efficient robustness (Claim 3 scatter plot). Paper positions perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension with strong empirical support.\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Routing helps but advantage is moderate — specialized experts outperform universal LoRA by a meaningful but not dominant margin, absolute performance clearly trails capacity-routing SOTA\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Specialized LoRA experts show advantage on 2 of 3 perturbation types (likely Visual and Physical), but Spatial expert underperforms due to layout perturbations requiring whole-trajectory reasoning beyond single-step LoRA correction\"\n      - \"Universal LoRA is a stronger baseline than expected — mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness\"\n      - \"PAR routing is accurate but soft routing dilutes correction signal compared to oracle hard routing (gap 3-6%)\"\n      - \"Absolute performance trails VLA-GSE by >8% due to pi0 backbone capacity ceiling, not routing quality\"\n    number_ranges:\n      libero_plus_overall_meta_brain: [69, 75]\n      gain_over_frozen: [15, 21]\n      universal_lora_overall: [63, 68]\n      gain_over_universal_lora: [4, 9]\n      per_type_specialization_delta_max: [6, 14]\n      oracle_par_gap: [3, 6]\n      routing_diagonality: [0.70, 0.85]\n      gain_per_million_params: [2.5, 3.5]\n      cm_ablation_delta: [0, 2]\n    narrative_direction: \"Claim 1 unchanged (Phase 0 already validated). Claim 2 reframed: 'routing by perturbation type improves adaptation efficiency, with strongest gains on perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages (visual, physical) vs. distributed ones (spatial)' — adds nuance rather than universal superiority. Claim 3 reframed as parameter-efficiency-per-gain: Meta-Brain achieves ~70% of VLA-GSE's absolute gain with 5% of parameters, positioning it as complementary (composable with capacity routing) rather than replacement. Ablation ladder still shows clear Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle ordering. Add analysis section explaining which perturbation types benefit most from routing and why — turns partial result into mechanistic insight. May need one additional experiment: combined Meta-Brain + universal LoRA residual to show routing is additive to uniform adaptation.\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Specialized LoRA barely outperforms or matches universal LoRA — core routing hypothesis does not produce meaningful downstream improvement despite perfect Phase 0 classification\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Universal LoRA is surprisingly effective: mixed-perturbation training implicitly learns perturbation-conditional representations without explicit routing\"\n      - \"LoRA rank-16 correction capacity is insufficient to produce differentiated type-specific behaviors — experts converge to similar solutions\"\n      - \"PAR routing weights are near-uniform regardless of perturbation type, indicating the routing signal doesn't translate to actionable correction difference\"\n      - \"Absolute performance ceiling on pi0+LIBERO-Plus is low (~65-70%) regardless of adaptation strategy, due to pi0 backbone limitations on this benchmark\"\n    number_ranges:\n      libero_plus_overall_meta_brain: [64, 69]\n      gain_over_frozen: [10, 15]\n      universal_lora_overall: [62, 67]\n      gain_over_universal_lora: [0, 4]\n      per_type_specialization_delta_max: [1, 6]\n      oracle_par_gap: [1, 5]\n      routing_diagonality: [0.45, 0.70]\n      gain_per_million_params: [1.7, 2.5]\n      cm_ablation_delta: [-1, 1]\n    narrative_direction: \"Core reframe: paper pivots from 'routing improves correction' to 'what VLA representations reveal about perturbation processing'. Lead contribution becomes the Phase 0 empirical finding — strongest result we have (F1=1.0 at K=1 single layer) — as a mechanistic discovery about VLA internal representations. Contribution 2 becomes the systematic design-dimension analysis: we tested perturbation-diagnostic routing and found that explicit routing yields marginal gains over implicit adaptation, providing evidence that LoRA mixed training already captures perturbation-conditional structure. This is a negative-but-informative result framed as understanding the landscape. Contribution 3: the framework itself (PAR + LoRA bank) as a modular, composable architecture that enables future work combining diagnostic routing with higher-capacity experts or multi-backbone settings. Title shifts from 'Meta-Brain: targeted correction via perturbation routing' to something like 'Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing'. Still targets CoRL — the Phase 0 finding + design dimension analysis is a solid empirical contribution, and honest negative results on routing inform the community. May add 6-class routing experiment to test whether finer granularity helps, and cross-backbone Phase 0 replication to strengthen the diagnostic finding as the central contribution.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10951,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10951},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"83893faf-9701-4293-9cba-5572cfa51016","uuid":"4ec99562-bbff-48ce-ae0c-7bf64d3a1f2a"}
